Beyond Materiality, something

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:06.370
we all need to try to do.

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:08.620
The head of the
Department of Architecture

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:13.330
here at MIT, Nader Tehrani, and
he will bring out the panel.

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:17.290
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:20.838
Thank you.

00:03:20.838 --> 00:03:22.130
Thank you for the introduction.

00:03:22.130 --> 00:03:25.550
Good evening, ladies
and gentlemen.

00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:27.280
Welcome to tonight's event.

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:29.620
It's a great pleasure
to be able to make

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:32.020
an introduction for
such an occasion,

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:35.050
as it represents
the best of MIT.

00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:38.680
A place that not only
promises to mediate

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:41.200
between the arts,
sciences, and technology,

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:45.460
but in fact, to draw out
its greatest potentials.

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:47.770
I do want to also thank
Leila Kinney for making this

00:03:47.770 --> 00:03:50.590
possible, and CAST
in general for making

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:53.410
this entire week possible.

00:03:53.410 --> 00:03:56.110
CAST has been at the forefront
of many such opportunities,

00:03:56.110 --> 00:03:58.450
and we in architecture
have benefited a great deal

00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:00.670
from their broad
vision in helping

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:02.150
us to expand our culture.

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:04.760
This is great.

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:07.940
Before I introduce
tonight's speaker,

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:10.850
I would like to first welcome
Anton Garcia-Abril who

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:14.060
will, alongside me,
help to moderate and act

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:16.829
as interlocutor for the evening.

00:04:16.829 --> 00:04:20.560
It is no accident that he
is here with us tonight.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:22.750
Anton just joined the
faculty of architecture

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:26.380
and promises to bring great
maturity and sophistication

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:28.820
to the arena of design
and building technology

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:32.320
specifically within
our curriculum.

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:35.170
With his team and
ensemble and their POPLab,

00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:36.970
Anton's presence
has already been

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:40.097
felt in the halls of
3rd semester core,

00:04:40.097 --> 00:04:41.680
and we've all had
the honor of hearing

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:44.110
him speak about his
own work and research

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:46.820
for the open house
just two weeks ago.

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:49.700
Anton's presence is known
on the global stage,

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:53.030
and his work is widely
recognized for its invention,

00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:55.340
its rigor, and play.

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:59.960
His focus on material agency
and its corollary methods

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:01.580
of fabrication have
brought new life

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:08.690
to ancient ways of conceiving
building protocols while they

00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:12.890
have also brought new depth to
modern construction systems.

00:05:12.890 --> 00:05:17.540
Of the many protean ways he has
operated on material systems,

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:21.440
maybe his reassessment of
masonry and concrete structures

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:24.830
remain the most
alarming and seductive.

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:28.880
In an era where we can only
conceive of stone as veneers,

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:33.740
as finishes, or as ornaments,
Anton has brought new weight

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:35.550
to the medium.

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:39.130
His Music Studies
Center, The Truffle,

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:42.090
and the SGA headquarters
are but three examples

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:45.690
that bear witness to the
astonishing ways in which

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:50.370
his studio has reinvigorated
a discourse on stereotomy,

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:53.610
on the monolithic,
and on the colossal--

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:56.040
not to mention the monumental.

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:58.230
While these were categories
previously reserved

00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:00.690
for the arena of
history and theory,

00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:02.790
looking back at the
Neolithic period--

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:06.570
or more recently, if you
like, towards the Romanesque--

00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:09.510
I'm reminded of the way
in which all of this

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:12.600
was theorized by Rodolphe
el-Khoury in his book,

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:15.360
On the Monolith, in the 1990s.

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:17.970
Examining the way in which
contemporary buildings are

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:22.110
revisiting one of architecture's
core figurative strategies

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:25.560
to inspire complex ways of
transforming both building

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:28.440
culture, and perception today.

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:31.470
If anything, Anton's
work radicalizes what

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:34.030
el-Khoury begins to theorize.

00:06:34.030 --> 00:06:37.810
Anton's own second
doctoral thesis revolves

00:06:37.810 --> 00:06:41.080
around stressed mass,
bringing our attention

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:44.470
not only to the ways in which
engineering has advanced

00:06:44.470 --> 00:06:47.110
structural agency,
but also how this

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:51.160
has enabled architectural
strategies that require mass

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:53.710
as the basis of
their transformation,

00:06:53.710 --> 00:06:56.950
their communication,
and function.

00:06:56.950 --> 00:07:02.140
Anton's architecture is
serious, but it's also deadpan.

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:04.630
There's no other way of
interpreting his Truffle,

00:07:04.630 --> 00:07:07.480
especially in the way
that it is delivered

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:10.870
in his now infamous video
of its construction.

00:07:10.870 --> 00:07:15.680
It has gravitas and weights,
metaphorically and literally,

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:19.000
but also the lightness
of being, which

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:22.660
brings us to the main
introduction of the evening.

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:25.180
Tonight's speaker,
Tomas Saraceno,

00:07:25.180 --> 00:07:29.170
is an expert on lightness,
but with the same gravitas

00:07:29.170 --> 00:07:32.980
and weight of our
interlocutor, Anton.

00:07:32.980 --> 00:07:35.710
Saraceno's structures
are ephemeral,

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:40.330
they're intricate, immaterial,
and stubbornly light,

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:43.870
refusing to be subordinated
by the traditional mandates

00:07:43.870 --> 00:07:46.480
of tectonics.

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:48.910
Not innocent to the
protocols of architecture,

00:07:48.910 --> 00:07:51.460
this artist, if it
is OK to reduce him

00:07:51.460 --> 00:07:55.250
to such a nomenclature,
is, in fact, an architect.

00:07:55.250 --> 00:07:57.800
A graduate of the
Universidad de Buenos Aires

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:00.470
with a postgraduate degree
in art and architecture

00:08:00.470 --> 00:08:05.000
in Escuela Superior de Bellas
Artes Ernesto de la Carcova.

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:07.850
And then, again, another
postgraduate degree

00:08:07.850 --> 00:08:11.360
from the Staatliche
Hochschule in Frankfurt.

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:16.340
Tomas is, if anything,
overeducated in architecture.

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:19.400
Something that gave way to his
interest in the arts, but also

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:23.330
offered an alternative path
for architectural speculation.

00:08:23.330 --> 00:08:25.100
And this is important.

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:27.950
I like to say that
Saraceno had to escape

00:08:27.950 --> 00:08:30.530
the architectural
profession in order

00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:33.200
to undertake a more
challenging and engaged

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:35.000
architectural practice.

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:39.409
But a practice that had as
one of its core ambitions

00:08:39.409 --> 00:08:42.980
and motivations to
demonstrate the very power

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:46.280
of the architectural discipline
and its ability to transform

00:08:46.280 --> 00:08:49.850
reality as we know it today.

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:52.160
I will not belabor an
analysis of his work.

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:55.040
He will have the time to
make his case himself.

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:58.280
However, it may help
to situate his work.

00:08:58.280 --> 00:09:00.650
As everybody knows,
the architectural field

00:09:00.650 --> 00:09:03.410
has itself opened up many
alternative practices

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:05.960
in the past two
decades, if not more.

00:09:05.960 --> 00:09:09.260
With the installation becoming
one of its main protagonists,

00:09:09.260 --> 00:09:11.000
having even been
institutionalized,

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:12.980
not only in various
curricula as we

00:09:12.980 --> 00:09:16.310
have here, but also
memorialized in annual events

00:09:16.310 --> 00:09:18.650
such as the PS1 Series.

00:09:18.650 --> 00:09:22.100
The work of Saraceno may draw
attraction from this tradition,

00:09:22.100 --> 00:09:25.010
but it also should not
be confused with it.

00:09:25.010 --> 00:09:27.740
While his work is
parasitical to architecture,

00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:30.260
its best function
is to challenge

00:09:30.260 --> 00:09:35.150
the very institution of art and
architecture at the same time.

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:39.980
His most recent work in Milan on
spacetime foam is an exemplary

00:09:39.980 --> 00:09:42.920
piece of work, in that
beyond the material nature

00:09:42.920 --> 00:09:45.530
of the installation--
which is, in its own right,

00:09:45.530 --> 00:09:47.240
quite sophisticated--

00:09:47.240 --> 00:09:50.900
it is an assault on perception,
sensation, and effect

00:09:50.900 --> 00:09:53.750
on the one hand, but
also on the other,

00:09:53.750 --> 00:09:58.070
a challenge to the very
building type which is its host.

00:09:58.070 --> 00:10:00.650
To the codes that
regulate the building,

00:10:00.650 --> 00:10:05.010
to the social foundations that
form how we use that space

00:10:05.010 --> 00:10:06.770
and how it's negotiated.

00:10:06.770 --> 00:10:08.840
In the tradition of
Christo and Jeanne-Claude,

00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:10.970
one could claim that the
work is not only invested

00:10:10.970 --> 00:10:14.120
in the architectural, but
also in the political and

00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:17.180
bureaucratic protocols that
define, limit, and give

00:10:17.180 --> 00:10:20.060
potential to the
practice, whether that's

00:10:20.060 --> 00:10:22.160
the practice of
architecture or art.

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:24.380
However, in the
hands of Saraceno,

00:10:24.380 --> 00:10:27.110
these parameters are led
to dizzying and vertiginous

00:10:27.110 --> 00:10:30.680
results beyond that
of his predecessors.

00:10:30.680 --> 00:10:32.570
With a disarming
character, Saraceno

00:10:32.570 --> 00:10:35.060
is not only at ease
with his imagination,

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:37.650
but also with that
of his collaborators.

00:10:37.650 --> 00:10:40.370
A militant collaborator,
each project

00:10:40.370 --> 00:10:43.250
is precisely composed
of a variety of players

00:10:43.250 --> 00:10:45.980
from different disciplines--
from the arts, the sciences,

00:10:45.980 --> 00:10:47.660
and way beyond.

00:10:47.660 --> 00:10:49.610
He operates with
methodologies that

00:10:49.610 --> 00:10:51.530
are at once systemic
in their questions,

00:10:51.530 --> 00:10:55.670
but also open to empirical
speculation, research, failure,

00:10:55.670 --> 00:10:57.080
and risk.

00:10:57.080 --> 00:11:00.560
Each project is an exercise
that has not yet been

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:01.940
rehearsed or choreographed.

00:11:01.940 --> 00:11:04.760
Much of it is tweaked
and recalibrated

00:11:04.760 --> 00:11:07.640
on site with an eye
towards public engagement

00:11:07.640 --> 00:11:09.540
and interaction.

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:11.250
His work awaits you.

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:14.820
With a repulsion towards
the decorum of tour guided

00:11:14.820 --> 00:11:17.130
protocols, his
attitude is formed

00:11:17.130 --> 00:11:20.970
around pure physical
engagement and negotiation,

00:11:20.970 --> 00:11:23.400
as if to use architecture
and materiality

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:27.070
to challenge social convention.

00:11:27.070 --> 00:11:31.690
And maybe, it is exactly the
frame of the art institution

00:11:31.690 --> 00:11:36.040
that can offer the suspension
of disbelief to help radicalize,

00:11:36.040 --> 00:11:39.430
not so much the mise
en scene of Saraceno,

00:11:39.430 --> 00:11:42.220
but his veritable
commitment to inventing

00:11:42.220 --> 00:11:44.110
new architectural frameworks.

00:11:44.110 --> 00:11:46.240
So without further
ado, I have to say

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:49.720
it is hard to imagine a more
appropriate visitor to MIT

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:52.090
Culture for this
occasion, and please

00:11:52.090 --> 00:11:54.520
help me to give a
warm welcome to Tomas.

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:57.957
[APPLAUSE]

00:12:07.060 --> 00:12:10.460
Well, thank you
for the invitation.

00:12:10.460 --> 00:12:11.800
It's working, yes?

00:12:11.800 --> 00:12:12.470
The microphone?

00:12:12.470 --> 00:12:12.970
Yes.

00:12:15.940 --> 00:12:19.090
I have to go from the beginning.

00:12:19.090 --> 00:12:21.630
Let me put it here.

00:12:21.630 --> 00:12:22.950
One second.

00:12:22.950 --> 00:12:24.803
And then--

00:12:24.803 --> 00:12:25.845
Tomas, I lowered the mic.

00:12:25.845 --> 00:12:27.540
You may want to bring
it to your height.

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:29.230
But I think so-- ah.

00:12:29.230 --> 00:12:29.890
Hello?

00:12:29.890 --> 00:12:30.540
Hello?

00:12:30.540 --> 00:12:31.320
Yes.

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:32.700
Now it's working.

00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:37.800
And, no, a little bit like,
we said maybe the lights,

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:40.990
we will leave them
off a little bit.

00:12:40.990 --> 00:12:42.900
If somebody want to sleep.

00:12:42.900 --> 00:12:44.220
That's a good idea, no?

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:47.070
At least I don't see you.

00:12:47.070 --> 00:12:50.180
But, OK, that's good.

00:12:50.180 --> 00:12:50.680
No.

00:12:50.680 --> 00:12:55.290
And then the other thing is,
like, I would like to say,

00:12:55.290 --> 00:12:57.870
I mean, thank you for
all the beautiful week,

00:12:57.870 --> 00:13:01.440
for all the beautiful words
that I have heard today

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:02.820
and during this week.

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:06.450
And I mean, the list of
things is, I would say,

00:13:06.450 --> 00:13:07.750
really endless.

00:13:07.750 --> 00:13:09.720
And how inspiring I
have been to be here

00:13:09.720 --> 00:13:11.700
and still what is to come.

00:13:11.700 --> 00:13:13.350
But nevertheless,
I mean, I think

00:13:13.350 --> 00:13:15.930
today is a little bit,
I will try to say,

00:13:15.930 --> 00:13:18.690
like it's more like, again,
a kind of experiment.

00:13:18.690 --> 00:13:21.030
Let's say, I don't like to
make presentation and try

00:13:21.030 --> 00:13:21.990
to explain my work.

00:13:21.990 --> 00:13:24.690
Or it's more about maybe
what had come up to you

00:13:24.690 --> 00:13:27.210
and what you can
imagine about it.

00:13:27.210 --> 00:13:30.180
Maybe it's a trigger of
some kind of imagination.

00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:34.200
But I think that all of us
have somehow here or there.

00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:37.710
And then it's like maybe the
capability to build something.

00:13:37.710 --> 00:13:39.343
And somehow together.

00:13:39.343 --> 00:13:41.010
You know, I always
thought like, I mean,

00:13:41.010 --> 00:13:44.220
I kind of like to put
[INAUDIBLE] myself

00:13:44.220 --> 00:13:44.880
in this case.

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:47.710
You know, I know I have to
stick it to half an hour.

00:13:47.710 --> 00:13:50.010
This means it is now 6:52.

00:13:50.010 --> 00:13:51.480
I have to get there.

00:13:51.480 --> 00:13:55.410
But I have like, I
think, some 400 slides.

00:13:55.410 --> 00:13:57.600
I always thought like
a way to interact

00:13:57.600 --> 00:14:01.380
between me which is here, which
is kind of somehow boring,

00:14:01.380 --> 00:14:06.030
also, but it's to give to all
of you kind of a remote control.

00:14:06.030 --> 00:14:09.330
And then we know that in half an
hour, we have to go through it.

00:14:09.330 --> 00:14:12.030
