also try to build only one.

00:38:08.920 --> 00:38:11.680
Here we are, computer
tomography, try to read it.

00:38:11.680 --> 00:38:14.660
The thickness of the
spider web is 0.00.

00:38:14.660 --> 00:38:18.430
It's 1,000 of a millimeter,
and the best machines

00:38:18.430 --> 00:38:19.360
that we could find.

00:38:19.360 --> 00:38:23.110
And I think that have
no best, not micro CT

00:38:23.110 --> 00:38:28.930
scan, but kind of this scale or
the dimension of a spider web.

00:38:28.930 --> 00:38:30.130
You cannot read it.

00:38:30.130 --> 00:38:33.100
This means we start to ask
to a lot of laboratories.

00:38:33.100 --> 00:38:37.000
And one of the more
specialized, if I'm not wrong,

00:38:37.000 --> 00:38:41.740
is [INAUDIBLE] from a
university in Basel.

00:38:41.740 --> 00:38:44.770
He's a very keen person
on understanding.

00:38:44.770 --> 00:38:46.360
What they were
doing, try to also,

00:38:46.360 --> 00:38:48.340
I mean, spider
webs, you can always

00:38:48.340 --> 00:38:50.380
divide it in bi-dimension
and three dimension.

00:38:50.380 --> 00:38:53.560
Kind of not very scientific,
but it's kind of used, always.

00:38:53.560 --> 00:38:55.180
If it's never
bi-dimension because it

00:38:55.180 --> 00:38:56.620
involves some variation.

00:38:56.620 --> 00:38:58.870
But what we figured
it out that nobody

00:38:58.870 --> 00:39:02.530
have ever scan a three
dimensional spider web, or kind

00:39:02.530 --> 00:39:04.090
of digitalized, or
try to understand

00:39:04.090 --> 00:39:08.020
more precise the merging
properties of a three

00:39:08.020 --> 00:39:09.105
dimensional spider web.

00:39:09.105 --> 00:39:11.230
This means, you know, the
exhibition was coming up.

00:39:11.230 --> 00:39:13.210
You have to present it
in the Venice Biennale,

00:39:13.210 --> 00:39:15.880
was this invitation,
and we couldn't

00:39:15.880 --> 00:39:18.820
find even a method to
start to scan it for then

00:39:18.820 --> 00:39:20.500
be able to rebuild it.

00:39:20.500 --> 00:39:23.530
This mean one day what we
were doing-- ah, here--

00:39:23.530 --> 00:39:26.070
was with the laser scan, just
illuminate one of the boxes.

00:39:26.070 --> 00:39:27.820
And then when you
illuminate with a scans,

00:39:27.820 --> 00:39:29.740
you can see exactly
the intersection point

00:39:29.740 --> 00:39:31.875
with a laser and the web.

00:39:31.875 --> 00:39:33.250
This is mean based
on these, then

00:39:33.250 --> 00:39:38.620
we went to the
Technical University

00:39:38.620 --> 00:39:41.320
in Darmstadt, to the
Photogrammetry Institute,

00:39:41.320 --> 00:39:43.750
and then we asked
them to kind of help

00:39:43.750 --> 00:39:48.250
us to make this photogrammetry
system where we will

00:39:48.250 --> 00:39:50.450
take in parallel into machine.

00:39:50.450 --> 00:39:53.830
I mean, this is a project we
took two years to realize this.

00:39:53.830 --> 00:39:56.660
Was quite a long process.

00:39:56.660 --> 00:39:58.420
Here are some of
the slides, yet are

00:39:58.420 --> 00:40:01.390
all these parts of the scan.

00:40:01.390 --> 00:40:04.270
And then even we were trying
to make many scripting

00:40:04.270 --> 00:40:06.730
in the computer and
try to kind of connect

00:40:06.730 --> 00:40:11.650
some of and the threats.

00:40:11.650 --> 00:40:13.990
And finally, we have
to do it all by hand,

00:40:13.990 --> 00:40:17.290
because it was never working
so properly, the scripting.

00:40:17.290 --> 00:40:19.360
And then based on this,
then, what they did

00:40:19.360 --> 00:40:22.210
was kind of somehow building it.

00:40:22.210 --> 00:40:26.260
Mean we got this kind of
enormous amounts of numbers

00:40:26.260 --> 00:40:29.020
and nodes and intersections.

00:40:29.020 --> 00:40:33.840
And then, I mean, we
can drive then, later,

00:40:33.840 --> 00:40:34.900
exactly how we do it.

00:40:34.900 --> 00:40:36.358
But you know, every
time that there

00:40:36.358 --> 00:40:40.440
is two or more lengths reaching
to a point, we put a vertical.

00:40:40.440 --> 00:40:44.820
We print all the nodes on
the floor and on the ceiling.

00:40:44.820 --> 00:40:47.850
This means we know exactly
where the nodes are happening,

00:40:47.850 --> 00:40:52.470
and then we put a nylon line at
the high where the nodes are,

00:40:52.470 --> 00:40:54.930
and then we start to
build from one node

00:40:54.930 --> 00:40:56.850
to another node in here.

00:40:56.850 --> 00:40:59.130
Because what had happened,
when you pull one thread,

00:40:59.130 --> 00:41:01.320
this thread might
bifurcating three and then

00:41:01.320 --> 00:41:02.360
four and then five.

00:41:02.360 --> 00:41:04.860
This means it's very difficult
if you don't have the exactly

00:41:04.860 --> 00:41:08.842
position on space all the time
where the nodes are located.

00:41:08.842 --> 00:41:10.550
It's very easy then
to pull into the form

00:41:10.550 --> 00:41:12.870
of the net in another position.

00:41:12.870 --> 00:41:15.000
This mean the nylon
threads, and then you

00:41:15.000 --> 00:41:17.580
have to navigate
through the web,

00:41:17.580 --> 00:41:20.608
also, which was
pretty complicated.

00:41:20.608 --> 00:41:22.150
Here you can see
mostly how was that.

00:41:22.150 --> 00:41:25.650
No, it come from the
floor, and somehow start

00:41:25.650 --> 00:41:29.020
to elevate up in a
three dimensional space.

00:41:29.020 --> 00:41:29.520
Yeah.

00:41:34.080 --> 00:41:35.370
Oh, it was endless work.

00:41:35.370 --> 00:41:36.720
I mean, it was winter outside.

00:41:36.720 --> 00:41:37.260
Snow.

00:41:37.260 --> 00:41:39.080
And then we have
to heat up the--

00:41:48.130 --> 00:41:50.800
and also, I mean, now I
think so, on Monday, I

00:41:50.800 --> 00:41:54.060
meet somebody here at MIT
who specializes on spiders.

00:41:54.060 --> 00:41:56.350
And I'm very
interesting to start

00:41:56.350 --> 00:41:57.820
to see and to share
some knowledge

00:41:57.820 --> 00:42:02.380
and maybe I can give them
the data of this spider web,

00:42:02.380 --> 00:42:04.690
and then see how it's going.

00:42:04.690 --> 00:42:07.060
Yeah, people were
able to enter, also.

00:42:07.060 --> 00:42:09.610
What had happened later,
it's like, I mean,

00:42:09.610 --> 00:42:11.650
there was many kind of
discipline working on it.

00:42:11.650 --> 00:42:14.170
Each of them make their
own scientific paper

00:42:14.170 --> 00:42:19.330
on the ones who kind of had
to develop kind of the system

00:42:19.330 --> 00:42:21.340
to make the scan.

00:42:21.340 --> 00:42:23.710
Peter Jaeger from the
Senckenberg Museum

00:42:23.710 --> 00:42:26.125
presented a project in
a paper in a Congress

00:42:26.125 --> 00:42:28.000
[INAUDIBLE] Congress of
acknowledging Poland.

00:42:28.000 --> 00:42:33.310
And then when I
met Jim Clement, he

00:42:33.310 --> 00:42:36.190
is responsible of a lot
of life science experiment

00:42:36.190 --> 00:42:38.350
on the International
Space Station

00:42:38.350 --> 00:42:40.120
when I was at NASA in 2009.

00:42:40.120 --> 00:42:42.745
And he said, oh, you managed to
scan a three dimensional spider

00:42:42.745 --> 00:42:43.245
web.

00:42:43.245 --> 00:42:44.080
I said, well, yeah.

00:42:44.080 --> 00:42:46.217
Why we don't send it to ISS, no?

00:42:46.217 --> 00:42:47.800
And then I said wow,
that's fantastic.

00:42:47.800 --> 00:42:51.970
Because somehow, in my kind
of send spider back to space

00:42:51.970 --> 00:42:53.020
and see how.

00:42:53.020 --> 00:42:54.370
Because before, it was not sent.

00:42:54.370 --> 00:42:58.060
I mean, spider had been in space
in a microgravity environment,

00:42:58.060 --> 00:43:00.520
but always bi-dimension,
never three-dimensions.

00:43:00.520 --> 00:43:01.990
They could not
really study them.

00:43:01.990 --> 00:43:04.390
They will not make sense,
maybe, to compare them.

00:43:04.390 --> 00:43:07.480
But somehow also what I,
and then we supply this

00:43:07.480 --> 00:43:09.020
and we keep going on.

00:43:09.020 --> 00:43:10.960
Now, there is somebody
in Japan, which

00:43:10.960 --> 00:43:14.260
is very obsessed about that
all kind of connection,

00:43:14.260 --> 00:43:17.050
planar, and not happening in
a three dimensional space.

00:43:17.050 --> 00:43:19.480
This means he's
writing another paper

00:43:19.480 --> 00:43:21.820
on kind of a different
interpretation

00:43:21.820 --> 00:43:23.080
of the same work.

00:43:23.080 --> 00:43:26.350
But what somehow it kind of
all the time kind of fascinated

00:43:26.350 --> 00:43:30.422
me is this idea,
you know, it's like,

00:43:30.422 --> 00:43:32.380
had the most beautiful
talk with Jerry Friedman

00:43:32.380 --> 00:43:35.048
the other day in his laboratory.

00:43:35.048 --> 00:43:37.090
And it's something that
he all the time also say.

00:43:37.090 --> 00:43:40.840
You know, like we know only
4% things that we have named.

00:43:40.840 --> 00:43:43.960
96% still is black
matter or dark energy,

00:43:43.960 --> 00:43:45.330
and all the rest, 4%.

00:43:45.330 --> 00:43:47.600
The human, the planets,
the hydrogen, the helium.

00:43:47.600 --> 00:43:49.420
And I mean, you may
know much better

00:43:49.420 --> 00:43:50.770
than me what's going on there.

00:43:50.770 --> 00:43:54.010
But somehow, the
analogy that people

00:43:54.010 --> 00:43:56.560
have found to try to explain
the origin of the universe

00:43:56.560 --> 00:43:59.170
is kind of a three dimensional
spider web, which somehow you

00:43:59.170 --> 00:44:03.010
can find in every corner of our
house, which at the same time

00:44:03.010 --> 00:44:07.300
was less studied, maybe, as we
know how the universe is done

00:44:07.300 --> 00:44:08.080
somehow.

00:44:08.080 --> 00:44:10.882
You know, I kind of like this
knowing, they are knowing,

00:44:10.882 --> 00:44:12.340
they are knowing,
they are knowing,

00:44:12.340 --> 00:44:13.882
the analogy that
you find, also, it's

00:44:13.882 --> 00:44:15.640
less known than what
you thought, also,

00:44:15.640 --> 00:44:17.260
at the beginning.

00:44:17.260 --> 00:44:22.010
Well, this will be
the papers, and, well,

00:44:22.010 --> 00:44:25.590
and this is one of
the last images, where

00:44:25.590 --> 00:44:29.270
we are kind of trying to build
again the space elevator based

00:44:29.270 --> 00:44:32.510
on some ideas down there.

00:44:32.510 --> 00:44:33.610
I think so, that's it.

00:44:33.610 --> 00:44:35.950
I think so more or
less I made it on time.

00:44:35.950 --> 00:44:36.951
Thank you.

00:44:36.951 --> 00:44:40.388
[APPLAUSE]

00:44:59.640 --> 00:45:03.400
[INAUDIBLE]

00:45:06.340 --> 00:45:07.930
Do you have your mic on?

00:45:07.930 --> 00:45:08.860
Oh, yes.

00:45:12.730 --> 00:45:16.270
Anton, I will let you
lead the questions,

00:45:16.270 --> 00:45:20.260
and then I will hold them back
from this side as you start.

00:45:23.110 --> 00:45:26.170
OK, Tomas.

00:45:26.170 --> 00:45:29.980
I think there's something
that you can't avoid.

00:45:29.980 --> 00:45:30.760
Yeah.

00:45:30.760 --> 00:45:33.920
You said you were
trained as an architect.

00:45:33.920 --> 00:45:37.360
So that leaves us some
questions about your my point

00:45:37.360 --> 00:45:39.130
of view main work environment.

00:45:39.130 --> 00:45:44.980
You operate in
space, but you're--

00:45:44.980 --> 00:45:46.420
Can you speak up, please?

00:45:46.420 --> 00:45:48.985
You're erasing part
of the, let's say,

00:45:48.985 --> 00:45:51.040
Vitruvian principles, no?

00:45:51.040 --> 00:45:55.300
The gravitas,
firmatis, utilitas.

00:45:55.300 --> 00:46:04.750
And in this reinterpretation
of our physical world

00:46:04.750 --> 00:46:09.370
in that slide line between
what's distinguished--

00:46:09.370 --> 00:46:14.110
our own vicissity and
the experience on these

00:46:14.110 --> 00:46:18.100
borders where you operate.

00:46:18.100 --> 00:46:21.730
My question is
that there's always

00:46:21.730 --> 00:46:27.970
the presence of the exist
in the real space, no?

00:46:27.970 --> 00:46:35.770
Somehow you need this presence
to express or to materialize

00:46:35.770 --> 00:46:37.097
the immaterial.

00:46:40.760 --> 00:46:41.512
[LAUGHS]

00:46:41.512 --> 00:46:42.345
That's the question.

00:46:47.630 --> 00:46:49.880
I think so, I get
more in trouble,

00:46:49.880 --> 00:46:52.768
also, like to define
what is material

00:46:52.768 --> 00:46:53.810
and what is not material.

00:46:53.810 --> 00:46:56.900
Like maybe one step backward.

00:46:56.900 --> 00:47:00.950
And then it's always
like, what is the process

00:47:00.950 --> 00:47:04.190
that you could invent
yourself to kind of delay

00:47:04.190 --> 00:47:07.190
this capability of perceiving?

00:47:07.190 --> 00:47:10.940
And to try to take into
account which are the players?

00:47:10.940 --> 00:47:13.250
And somehow, the
pieces somehow, also,

00:47:13.250 --> 00:47:17.090
kind of trigger this idea
of extending my capability,

00:47:17.090 --> 00:47:19.130
or maybe the capability
of the other ones,

00:47:19.130 --> 00:47:21.240
of how much role it plays.

00:47:25.280 --> 00:47:28.430
For example, in the
piece in Italy, I mean,

00:47:28.430 --> 00:47:29.150
it's very simple.

00:47:29.150 --> 00:47:33.140
But it's like, there are
some actors that put it

00:47:33.140 --> 00:47:41.300
on more in the way of how Bruno
Latour tried to define how much

00:47:41.300 --> 00:47:46.398
accountability of the
environment or in the--

00:47:46.398 --> 00:47:46.940
I don't know.

00:47:46.940 --> 00:47:48.500
I would just kind
of try to delay.

00:47:48.500 --> 00:47:51.680
I mean, now I forgot the writer.

00:47:51.680 --> 00:47:54.450
He was living in Argentina
for quite a long time.

00:47:54.450 --> 00:47:55.950
But he all the time
said like, look,

00:47:55.950 --> 00:47:58.100
if I managed to
concentrate on myself

00:47:58.100 --> 00:48:00.500
for 30 minutes just
looking at this glass,

00:48:00.500 --> 00:48:03.380
and now, and I do these
just to look at this,

00:48:03.380 --> 00:48:05.690
my life will be
completely different.

00:48:05.690 --> 00:48:08.420
And I completely agree on this.

00:48:08.420 --> 00:48:14.060
It's like, how much
can I extend myself,

00:48:14.060 --> 00:48:16.850
my ability to look
at something, right?

00:48:16.850 --> 00:48:18.980
And this, it might change again.

00:48:18.980 --> 00:48:21.740
And then it's like, I
don't know if I've really

00:48:21.740 --> 00:48:23.840
answered the question,
but that's something

00:48:23.840 --> 00:48:26.390
which can obsess me somehow.

00:48:26.390 --> 00:48:31.400
But to some degree, it
begs the follow-up question

00:48:31.400 --> 00:48:33.380
of who you think
your audience is.

00:48:33.380 --> 00:48:38.210
Often as architects, you
operate in two worlds.

00:48:38.210 --> 00:48:43.760
One, you build buildings for
the general public for people

00:48:43.760 --> 00:48:50.120
to work in, to rebel
in, to demonstrate in.

00:48:50.120 --> 00:48:51.620
In other words, it
has many publics.

00:48:51.620 --> 00:48:54.800
But another level of
discourse, one could say,

00:48:54.800 --> 00:48:55.980
is a meta-discourse.

00:48:55.980 --> 00:48:59.690
You design for a
particular audience.

00:48:59.690 --> 00:49:01.430
Even not even all architects.

00:49:01.430 --> 00:49:02.360
You may be--

00:49:02.360 --> 00:49:05.150
For spiders.

00:49:05.150 --> 00:49:08.480
Or for people or, I don't know.

00:49:08.480 --> 00:49:11.000
People who build
parallel universes.

00:49:11.000 --> 00:49:12.590
I don't know.

00:49:12.590 --> 00:49:18.050
Do you have an audience that
is internal to your circle?

00:49:18.050 --> 00:49:21.260
Whether it's art historical
or whether it's architects,

00:49:21.260 --> 00:49:27.740
but they are the bearers of a
certain discourse to which you

00:49:27.740 --> 00:49:29.780
compete with, aspired
to, et cetera?

00:49:35.180 --> 00:49:36.440
Not really, I think so.

00:49:36.440 --> 00:49:37.880
And I hope I will never have it.

00:49:37.880 --> 00:49:41.960
Like it's so
classified, somehow.

00:49:41.960 --> 00:49:44.300
It's like all the time,
you know, I hope so.

00:49:48.140 --> 00:49:48.740
How to say.

00:49:48.740 --> 00:49:52.550
It's like, well, I always
think, I'm kind of always

00:49:52.550 --> 00:49:55.910
fascinated you know how
internet have been invented.

00:49:55.910 --> 00:49:57.860
And let's put it
like this membrane,

00:49:57.860 --> 00:50:01.040
let's put it like it's more
like a platform for people

00:50:01.040 --> 00:50:04.250
to kind of engage, maybe, in
the construction of something.

00:50:04.250 --> 00:50:06.290
Which might be like a
kind of and architecture

00:50:06.290 --> 00:50:08.330
I'm interested in, and
kind of a social space

00:50:08.330 --> 00:50:13.100
who might engage people kind
of sharing some possibility

00:50:13.100 --> 00:50:16.430
of thinking about something.

00:50:16.430 --> 00:50:22.580
But the audience, I mean,
I'm audience all the time.

00:50:22.580 --> 00:50:24.660
You know, I cannot
separate so much.

00:50:24.660 --> 00:50:27.050
And also, I cannot separate
so much of disciplines.

00:50:27.050 --> 00:50:29.240
I'm kind of much more
interested to kind of read

00:50:29.240 --> 00:50:30.470
what is in between.

00:50:30.470 --> 00:50:32.460
Or let's say I'm not
so much interested.

00:50:32.460 --> 00:50:33.890
Oh, I'm an architect, an artist.

00:50:33.890 --> 00:50:35.810
Well, what we have
in common more.

00:50:35.810 --> 00:50:38.330
That we may operate
together and do something.

00:50:38.330 --> 00:50:40.600
That's what strike me much more.

00:50:40.600 --> 00:50:45.357
And what make us
do this spider web.

00:50:45.357 --> 00:50:47.690
I mean, the [INAUDIBLE] might
be interesting, something.

00:50:47.690 --> 00:50:49.190
I will have another sensibility.

00:50:49.190 --> 00:50:51.380
But somehow, we could
collaborate all together

00:50:51.380 --> 00:50:53.010
and manage to do it.

00:50:53.010 --> 00:50:56.420
And each of us, then the
scientist made the papers,

00:50:56.420 --> 00:50:59.660
and I do part of the exhibition
and something that is.

00:50:59.660 --> 00:51:03.680
But the audience, in this
case, was very diverse.

00:51:03.680 --> 00:51:06.680
And it was more
successful, let's say,

00:51:06.680 --> 00:51:08.420
I will feel myself
with how much we

00:51:08.420 --> 00:51:11.210
can engage in a kind
of a possible dialogue.

00:51:11.210 --> 00:51:13.045
And that's what, for
me, is architecture.

00:51:13.045 --> 00:51:15.170
Architecture, it's not like
the construction space.

00:51:15.170 --> 00:51:16.700
Architecture, you
can say, expanded

00:51:16.700 --> 00:51:18.200
to the construction of poetry.

00:51:18.200 --> 00:51:19.430
The architecture of poetry.

00:51:19.430 --> 00:51:22.460
The architecture of
a computer system.

00:51:22.460 --> 00:51:25.750
The architecture
of a musical piece.

00:51:25.750 --> 00:51:27.770
But you do make choices.

00:51:27.770 --> 00:51:29.270
For instance, at
some point, you had

00:51:29.270 --> 00:51:33.470
to decide whether the spider
web was this thin or that thick.

00:51:33.470 --> 00:51:36.020
So there are certain
a prioris that

00:51:36.020 --> 00:51:39.650
go with certain
specifications against which

00:51:39.650 --> 00:51:42.210
you critique yourself.

00:51:42.210 --> 00:51:45.200
And this invariably
involves a larger discourse.

00:51:45.200 --> 00:51:49.350
So basically, I'm asking
how you insert yourself

00:51:49.350 --> 00:51:54.840
into those critical
decision-making processes.

00:51:54.840 --> 00:51:59.220
I never do them alone, somehow.

00:51:59.220 --> 00:52:00.408
I hope so.

00:52:00.408 --> 00:52:02.700
I mean, the first thing, why
we choose the black widow,

00:52:02.700 --> 00:52:07.140
because at the
beginning, was supposed

00:52:07.140 --> 00:52:08.970
