What I remember is like, I mean,
at least in today's art scene,

01:04:32.500 --> 01:04:35.260
which I do not find maybe
so much the discourse

01:04:35.260 --> 01:04:37.530
into the architecture,
is this capability

01:04:37.530 --> 01:04:40.240
to the acceptance of
failure, which always Adorno

01:04:40.240 --> 01:04:44.860
said is one of the [INAUDIBLE].

01:04:44.860 --> 01:04:47.710
I mean it's something
that, [SPEAKING SPANISH],,

01:04:47.710 --> 01:04:50.500
you cannot put it
as a condition,

01:04:50.500 --> 01:04:52.990
but you cannot put
it anticipated,

01:04:52.990 --> 01:04:53.860
not put it on front.

01:04:53.860 --> 01:04:59.710
And I see many times into even
talking with artists mostly,

01:04:59.710 --> 01:05:01.240
more than architecture.

01:05:01.240 --> 01:05:04.480
Because maybe in art, maybe
because in architecture,

01:05:04.480 --> 01:05:07.000
you have the responsibility
that is a building

01:05:07.000 --> 01:05:08.110
that I have to stand.

01:05:08.110 --> 01:05:12.460
That there is something, that
the failure is something that,

01:05:12.460 --> 01:05:16.570
I mean, you cannot so
openly talk about it.

01:05:16.570 --> 01:05:18.490
But in the art world,
you know sometimes,

01:05:18.490 --> 01:05:22.150
and I'm trying to divide the
things that I didn't want to.

01:05:22.150 --> 01:05:26.090
But was this mostly
acceptance to say,

01:05:26.090 --> 01:05:28.750
well, it doesn't work well,
but you know, Adorno said,

01:05:28.750 --> 01:05:29.680
it could fail.

01:05:29.680 --> 01:05:31.450
And to be art, it have to fail.

01:05:31.450 --> 01:05:33.370
This is one of the
responsibility of, no?

01:05:33.370 --> 01:05:35.710
But you cannot put
it on the front.

01:05:35.710 --> 01:05:38.230
And I think so mostly for
me is more the ability

01:05:38.230 --> 01:05:41.980
to say, look, if I use
this pretext that it

01:05:41.980 --> 01:05:48.820
might be allowed
to fail, I'm not

01:05:48.820 --> 01:05:50.410
so persistent and consistent.

01:05:50.410 --> 01:05:54.550
And kind of, this means,
it's something which I--

01:05:54.550 --> 01:06:00.020
And this allowance to failure,
is it a matter of scale?

01:06:00.020 --> 01:06:04.990
Because now you are thinking
about 1.2 kilometer dome,

01:06:04.990 --> 01:06:08.890
flying structures.

01:06:08.890 --> 01:06:12.670
You're getting dangerously
close to architecture, right?

01:06:12.670 --> 01:06:14.290
Yes.

01:06:14.290 --> 01:06:15.330
Of the universe.

01:06:15.330 --> 01:06:16.900
Not of the cities.

01:06:16.900 --> 01:06:19.240
We don't want you
to be an architect.

01:06:19.240 --> 01:06:23.080
We want to stay
as you are, right?

01:06:23.080 --> 01:06:26.710
But there's a scale
of intervention,

01:06:26.710 --> 01:06:33.520
space modification of action,
that does not resist failure.

01:06:33.520 --> 01:06:36.670
No, I mean what I do
is like, how beautiful.

01:06:36.670 --> 01:06:40.407
I mean, and also, this was
a beautiful talk with you

01:06:40.407 --> 01:06:40.990
the other day.

01:06:40.990 --> 01:06:43.640
You know, it's like, there
is this kind of problem,

01:06:43.640 --> 01:06:47.200
let's say, of scales, again.

01:06:47.200 --> 01:06:51.350
But no.

01:06:54.560 --> 01:06:57.250
This was nice the other
day when we were talking.

01:06:57.250 --> 01:06:59.800
Like these kind of
1.2 miles diameter.

01:06:59.800 --> 01:07:01.872
This from one side,
where it pushed me a lot

01:07:01.872 --> 01:07:04.330
is like, hey, I want to make
it fly only with solar energy.

01:07:04.330 --> 01:07:07.210
And the sphere in this
case is the best thing.

01:07:07.210 --> 01:07:08.232
And here and there.

01:07:08.232 --> 01:07:10.190
But somehow, you know,
I always thinking, like,

01:07:10.190 --> 01:07:11.607
it would be so
beautiful that when

01:07:11.607 --> 01:07:14.170
we think about a city
as an aggregate of very

01:07:14.170 --> 01:07:16.840
simple and single houses.

01:07:16.840 --> 01:07:18.610
And that's the scale.

01:07:18.610 --> 01:07:20.410
Does it mean the
construction of the city

01:07:20.410 --> 01:07:23.630
will be the wish of
each single individual,

01:07:23.630 --> 01:07:26.170
you somehow generate
a big mountain.

01:07:26.170 --> 01:07:30.100
But again is the participation
of each single individual

01:07:30.100 --> 01:07:33.400
who somehow rely on the
responsibility of each of us

01:07:33.400 --> 01:07:35.710
toward the construction
of something.

01:07:35.710 --> 01:07:38.020
That has been a
city always, right?

01:07:38.020 --> 01:07:38.740
Well, yes.

01:07:38.740 --> 01:07:39.560
No!

01:07:39.560 --> 01:07:40.560
That's what I would say.

01:07:40.560 --> 01:07:42.910
Completely no.

01:07:42.910 --> 01:07:46.090
That's what have been the
city of the neglected.

01:07:46.090 --> 01:07:48.520
I mean, it's like, let's
say, my [? role, ?]

01:07:48.520 --> 01:07:50.350
put it on the
architectural scale,

01:07:50.350 --> 01:07:54.640
is like, how we could
engage people and citizens

01:07:54.640 --> 01:07:58.150
and everybody engaging
the participation

01:07:58.150 --> 01:07:59.740
of the urban planning.

01:07:59.740 --> 01:08:01.870
How we can convince
all of you that it's

01:08:01.870 --> 01:08:04.870
beautiful to build a city, and
how we can build it together.

01:08:04.870 --> 01:08:07.870
Now it is in the hands
of developers, [SCOFFS],,

01:08:07.870 --> 01:08:10.480
who sometimes make
horrible thing, money.

01:08:10.480 --> 01:08:13.570
There is a kind of a whole
horrible scene behind it.

01:08:13.570 --> 01:08:15.979
And very little people,
even architects,

01:08:15.979 --> 01:08:17.229
do not have so much influence.

01:08:17.229 --> 01:08:18.729
And for me, it's
about how we can

01:08:18.729 --> 01:08:21.550
spread or try to engage
people into the construction

01:08:21.550 --> 01:08:22.090
of a city?

01:08:22.090 --> 01:08:24.173
In this case, a flying
city, but we could do also,

01:08:24.173 --> 01:08:26.194
like, a normal city.

01:08:26.194 --> 01:08:27.069
You know what I mean?

01:08:27.069 --> 01:08:28.845
It's more about
invent the platform.

01:08:28.845 --> 01:08:30.220
Let's say, how
beautiful it would

01:08:30.220 --> 01:08:33.399
be if we managed to build
together as we did Linux,

01:08:33.399 --> 01:08:38.140
or as this improving
Wikipedia knowledge,

01:08:38.140 --> 01:08:41.350
we could somehow build a city.

01:08:41.350 --> 01:08:44.200
That somehow, it's
like, go back more to,

01:08:44.200 --> 01:08:46.930
let's say, Yona Friedman
architecture mobile.

01:08:46.930 --> 01:08:49.060
And, you know, there
is a tradition, also,

01:08:49.060 --> 01:08:51.870
into the architecture
scenario of trying

01:08:51.870 --> 01:08:57.740
to involve the people into the
use or misuse of something.

01:08:57.740 --> 01:08:59.500
And I think that's
really something

01:08:59.500 --> 01:09:04.569
which I would love to see
more on a different scale.

01:09:04.569 --> 01:09:05.529
Let me ask you this.

01:09:05.529 --> 01:09:09.220
If MIT were to offer you the
commission of a new building

01:09:09.220 --> 01:09:13.810
on campus with the full
engagement of our faculty

01:09:13.810 --> 01:09:18.520
and student cohorts, would
you accept the commission?

01:09:18.520 --> 01:09:19.029
Sure.

01:09:19.029 --> 01:09:20.569
[LAUGHTER]

01:09:20.569 --> 01:09:23.560
That's not even a question.

01:09:23.560 --> 01:09:26.096
But I would try to convince
you to make it fly.

01:09:26.096 --> 01:09:29.358
[LAUGHTER]

01:09:31.689 --> 01:09:33.729
No, but I think there
will be no beautiful--

01:09:33.729 --> 01:09:36.370
I mean, I would [INAUDIBLE].

01:09:36.370 --> 01:09:40.390
Now we kind of start to breathe,
breathe, breathe, we go up,

01:09:40.390 --> 01:09:43.000
we go up, we go up, this
kind of floating [INAUDIBLE]..

01:09:43.000 --> 01:09:45.640
We exhale hot air somehow.

01:09:45.640 --> 01:09:47.620
Or it's like a building.

01:09:47.620 --> 01:09:51.819
You come up in the
morning and you say, oh,

01:09:51.819 --> 01:09:53.205
which temperature is today?

01:09:53.205 --> 01:09:55.330
And you see the building
is like so high or so low,

01:09:55.330 --> 01:09:58.420
because it's like a
thermometer in the city which

01:09:58.420 --> 01:10:00.040
goes up and down.

01:10:00.040 --> 01:10:01.960
Or when you get
tired of MIT, you

01:10:01.960 --> 01:10:05.663
can just take off and
go somewhere else.

01:10:05.663 --> 01:10:07.830
But the other thing also,
what really drive me crazy

01:10:07.830 --> 01:10:12.585
in Germany is now is getting
the winter and a bad weather.

01:10:12.585 --> 01:10:13.960
This means sometimes
people come,

01:10:13.960 --> 01:10:17.500
and so you go to Palma de
Mallorca, beautiful weather.

01:10:17.500 --> 01:10:19.390
But something you
forgot it's like,

01:10:19.390 --> 01:10:22.060
just a few kilometers above,
where the same spot you are,

01:10:22.060 --> 01:10:24.580
is a super sunny day.

01:10:24.580 --> 01:10:25.990
Not so far, right?

01:10:25.990 --> 01:10:27.580
And then people
travel horizontally.

01:10:27.580 --> 01:10:31.740
But the vertical component
is missing, somehow.

01:10:31.740 --> 01:10:33.730
I tell you like a
very small story.

01:10:33.730 --> 01:10:36.730
I was in St. Petersburg
for three weeks,

01:10:36.730 --> 01:10:38.830
and there was no sun at all.

01:10:38.830 --> 01:10:41.420
No sun, no sun, no sun.

01:10:41.420 --> 01:10:43.870
Then I got in the
plane, finally out.

01:10:43.870 --> 01:10:46.060
And then usually, in
Argentina, we still,

01:10:46.060 --> 01:10:48.540
when the plane is
landing, we used to clap.

01:10:48.540 --> 01:10:51.970
There is some kind of
sensibility for [INAUDIBLE]..

01:10:51.970 --> 01:10:54.775
But when it happened,
the plane, it

01:10:54.775 --> 01:10:57.850
crossed the level of the clouds,
and people started to clap.

01:10:57.850 --> 01:11:00.655
They saw the sun, hey!

01:11:00.655 --> 01:11:01.780
And then I say, oh, my god.

01:11:01.780 --> 01:11:03.055
That's the first participant.

01:11:03.055 --> 01:11:04.930
You know, with them, I
would not have problem

01:11:04.930 --> 01:11:08.080
to convince them that I'm
doing a cloud [INAUDIBLE]..

01:11:08.080 --> 01:11:09.470
Above the level of the cloud.

01:11:13.060 --> 01:11:17.200
Tomas, you fly when you're
working with the public.

01:11:17.200 --> 01:11:21.010
May I use this opportunity to
open questions to your public

01:11:21.010 --> 01:11:21.880
out here?

01:11:21.880 --> 01:11:24.190
There are two microphones,
one on this side

01:11:24.190 --> 01:11:25.300
and one on the other side.

01:11:25.300 --> 01:11:28.430
And help to open
up this discussion.

01:11:28.430 --> 01:11:28.930
Thank you.

01:11:37.305 --> 01:11:38.430
Thank you very much, Tomas.

01:11:38.430 --> 01:11:40.200
This is fantastic.

01:11:40.200 --> 01:11:42.000
I'm not an artist.

01:11:42.000 --> 01:11:43.020
I'm not an architect.

01:11:43.020 --> 01:11:45.360
I did see your
exhibit in Berlin.

01:11:45.360 --> 01:11:47.910
And getting back to the
original question about who's

01:11:47.910 --> 01:11:49.320
your audience.

01:11:49.320 --> 01:11:51.030
To me, the audience
were the people

01:11:51.030 --> 01:11:53.430
who were participating
in that exhibit.

01:11:53.430 --> 01:11:56.310
Climbing into the
spheres, looking at it,

01:11:56.310 --> 01:11:58.960
experiencing
unmitigated delight.

01:11:58.960 --> 01:12:02.250
And I think that what's really
important for me, at least--

01:12:02.250 --> 01:12:05.460
and I had no idea about your
background as an architect--

01:12:05.460 --> 01:12:08.600
is your creation of
opportunities for delight.

01:12:08.600 --> 01:12:09.540
For the--

01:12:09.540 --> 01:12:10.590
For delight.

01:12:10.590 --> 01:12:11.730
Delight.

01:12:11.730 --> 01:12:13.620
No, delight as in--

01:12:13.620 --> 01:12:15.463
delight, joy, whatever.

01:12:15.463 --> 01:12:16.389
Ah, delight.

01:12:16.389 --> 01:12:17.278
Si, si.

01:12:17.278 --> 01:12:17.778
I'm sorry.

01:12:17.778 --> 01:12:18.704
My English.

01:12:18.704 --> 01:12:20.100
[LAUGHTER]

01:12:20.100 --> 01:12:21.455
Well, yeah, that too.

01:12:21.455 --> 01:12:23.200
The light as well.

01:12:23.200 --> 01:12:26.670
And the other thing, even
about the spider web,

01:12:26.670 --> 01:12:27.900
that was stunning to look.

01:12:27.900 --> 01:12:30.540
At and sort of seeing
the dependencies

01:12:30.540 --> 01:12:33.210
and interdependencies
among the various threads.

01:12:33.210 --> 01:12:36.210
So examining or
experiencing your work

01:12:36.210 --> 01:12:41.160
and thinking of from the
point of view of an audience,

01:12:41.160 --> 01:12:43.020
from the point of
view of a participant,

01:12:43.020 --> 01:12:45.870
you did mention social
participation to a degree.

01:12:45.870 --> 01:12:50.430
But to me, that is like,
looms as number one,

01:12:50.430 --> 01:12:52.530
and is what makes your work--

01:12:52.530 --> 01:12:54.270
that I've seen and
that I read about,

01:12:54.270 --> 01:12:57.300
the project you did Frankfurt
at the Russ Market--

01:12:57.300 --> 01:12:58.300
extraordinary.

01:12:58.300 --> 01:13:02.858
And I really hope that you
can continue to focus on that.

01:13:02.858 --> 01:13:04.650
The issues around
whether it's architecture

01:13:04.650 --> 01:13:07.650
or whether it's
whatever to me are,

01:13:07.650 --> 01:13:12.100
this may be not the right thing
to say right now, irrelevant.

01:13:23.780 --> 01:13:26.540
Your work takes my
breath away, and I'm just

01:13:26.540 --> 01:13:29.390
like, touched by your work.

01:13:29.390 --> 01:13:32.120
But I would like to
know how long someone

01:13:32.120 --> 01:13:36.920
is able to occupy your space.

01:13:36.920 --> 01:13:40.310
How long can you stay?

01:13:40.310 --> 01:13:41.540
In--

01:13:41.540 --> 01:13:42.320
In the web.

01:13:45.050 --> 01:13:46.850
In which space?

01:13:46.850 --> 01:13:49.910
In your space where
the people are.

01:13:49.910 --> 01:13:51.230
I'm not sure.

01:13:51.230 --> 01:13:52.920
That one, Italy, one
at the beginning?

01:13:52.920 --> 01:13:54.410
Yeah, any one.

01:13:54.410 --> 01:13:58.580
How many hours, days,
or, can someone--

01:13:58.580 --> 01:14:03.230
I always thought this
museum during the night

01:14:03.230 --> 01:14:05.060
did not have really
like a function.

01:14:05.060 --> 01:14:06.560
And I say, why we
don't make it kind

01:14:06.560 --> 01:14:09.480
of a hostel or a hotel
that people who go there?

01:14:09.480 --> 01:14:10.640
It's like a huge mattress.

01:14:10.640 --> 01:14:13.280
We could sleep all together.

01:14:13.280 --> 01:14:17.700
But usually, I mean,
they have a timing.

01:14:17.700 --> 01:14:21.200
It's like a half an hour
you can be up there.

01:14:21.200 --> 01:14:25.248
And then otherwise, you know.

01:14:25.248 --> 01:14:25.790
I don't know.

01:14:25.790 --> 01:14:28.310
I don't know what
will happen, actually.

01:14:28.310 --> 01:14:32.580
But there is a timing that you
get inside, you get a number,

01:14:32.580 --> 01:14:35.180
and then for half an hour you
can be there, and then go down.

01:14:35.180 --> 01:14:38.360
And then but always
we say like on the top

01:14:38.360 --> 01:14:40.400
or one of the levels.

01:14:40.400 --> 01:14:42.980
But on the bottom, you can
stay as long as you want.

01:14:42.980 --> 01:14:45.870
This mean also, you know, my
mother never went up there,

01:14:45.870 --> 01:14:46.370
you know.

01:14:46.370 --> 01:14:51.050
And she always
[INAUDIBLE] up, down, yes.

01:14:51.050 --> 01:14:51.590
Thank you.

01:14:57.780 --> 01:15:00.740
In your conversation
with Nadia and Anton

01:15:00.740 --> 01:15:06.330
before, you keep like resisting
your work from architecture.

01:15:06.330 --> 01:15:09.810
So I'm just curious about,
because in your work,

01:15:09.810 --> 01:15:13.120
you present a really strong
connection with architecture,

01:15:13.120 --> 01:15:13.620
actually.

01:15:13.620 --> 01:15:18.240
Like to share the idea of
social space, their gravity,

01:15:18.240 --> 01:15:20.070
their public engagement.

01:15:20.070 --> 01:15:23.130
And this idea has
just come across,

01:15:23.130 --> 01:15:24.720
like the art in architecture.

01:15:24.720 --> 01:15:27.180
And I'm just
wondering, how do you

01:15:27.180 --> 01:15:33.060
think your previous architecture
education contributed

01:15:33.060 --> 01:15:34.200
to your work?

01:15:34.200 --> 01:15:39.150
Or in another way
put it, how do you

01:15:39.150 --> 01:15:41.730
think your work will,
in turn, contribute

01:15:41.730 --> 01:15:43.660
to other architectural approach?

01:15:48.935 --> 01:15:49.810
I don't know so much.

01:15:49.810 --> 01:15:52.660
But maybe you know
much better than me.

01:15:56.410 --> 01:15:57.530
Maybe you can tell me.

01:15:57.530 --> 01:16:03.890
I mean, we have been together
in the competition when we were

01:16:03.890 --> 01:16:05.030
with [? Skyler ?] together?

01:16:05.030 --> 01:16:07.090
No, you were not there.

01:16:07.090 --> 01:16:08.060
No?

01:16:08.060 --> 01:16:08.560
Oh no.

01:16:08.560 --> 01:16:09.220
I forgot now.

01:16:09.220 --> 01:16:10.180
I mixed it up.

01:16:10.180 --> 01:16:17.568
But what should I have said?

01:16:17.568 --> 01:16:18.110
I don't know.

01:16:31.870 --> 01:16:32.772
Next!

01:16:32.772 --> 01:16:37.200
[LAUGHTER]

01:16:39.670 --> 01:16:43.750
No, but-- you're an architect?

01:16:43.750 --> 01:16:46.040
Or you study architecture?

01:16:46.040 --> 01:16:46.540
Yeah.

01:16:46.540 --> 01:16:48.220
Yeah, super.

01:16:48.220 --> 01:16:51.890
I hope, so, we can influence
each other all the time.

01:16:51.890 --> 01:16:52.810
OK.

01:16:52.810 --> 01:16:54.280
[INAUDIBLE]

01:16:54.280 --> 01:16:58.030
We can build it together.

01:16:58.030 --> 01:16:59.710
No, I mean, what was?

01:16:59.710 --> 01:17:04.810
It's like, I was the
other day at a Media Lab

01:17:04.810 --> 01:17:09.790
also trying to convince them
to help me on get some sense,

01:17:09.790 --> 01:17:11.590
not to measure the
temperature of people

01:17:11.590 --> 01:17:13.210
when they're up there.

01:17:13.210 --> 01:17:15.100
Not because the Media
Lab, also, there

01:17:15.100 --> 01:17:17.770
is this kind of strange
intersection, also,

01:17:17.770 --> 01:17:21.100
of how they put all these
sensor on the building up there.

01:17:21.100 --> 01:17:24.910
And then and how you
can track conversational

01:17:24.910 --> 01:17:25.660
[? temperature. ?]

01:17:25.660 --> 01:17:28.450
And that was, for me,
will be pretty useful too.

01:17:28.450 --> 01:17:31.300
Because the problem is like
when you have to rescue someone.

01:17:31.300 --> 01:17:33.010
But it never
happened yet, right?

01:17:33.010 --> 01:17:34.450
But in case.

01:17:34.450 --> 01:17:36.640
And also, it's like,
how much information

01:17:36.640 --> 01:17:41.320
you give to the person to be
able to move into that space.

01:17:41.320 --> 01:17:46.000
Now let's say on this
big air and moving.

01:17:46.000 --> 01:17:47.890
I mean, usually,
what I always tend

01:17:47.890 --> 01:17:51.603
to say is like, you don't
have to say nothing to them.

01:17:51.603 --> 01:17:53.770
Let's put it that I'm trying
to answer your question

01:17:53.770 --> 01:17:55.140
on architecture, no?

01:17:55.140 --> 01:17:59.280
It's more about
which information

01:17:59.280 --> 01:18:02.170
is necessary to
give it to somebody.

01:18:02.170 --> 01:18:06.870
And if it's necessary to
use or misuse the space.

01:18:06.870 --> 01:18:12.390
In that way, in that space
that was created, was there,

01:18:12.390 --> 01:18:15.510
usually they are
very severe with me.

01:18:15.510 --> 01:18:16.950
They tell, look,
the public first.

01:18:16.950 --> 01:18:19.740
Take out your shoes, take out
everything from your pocket,

01:18:19.740 --> 01:18:23.110
because it might make a hole
and you fall down 20 meters.

01:18:23.110 --> 01:18:24.150
Don't go to the border.

01:18:24.150 --> 01:18:25.530
Don't get together so much.

01:18:25.530 --> 01:18:26.890
Don't do this, don't do that.

01:18:26.890 --> 01:18:28.473
And at the end, it's
like, oh, my god.

01:18:28.473 --> 01:18:29.790
Don't tell this to the people.

01:18:29.790 --> 01:18:33.390
You know I hate this
kind of, it's a space

01:18:33.390 --> 01:18:34.530
that you might experience.

01:18:34.530 --> 01:18:36.420
And at the end, it's
contradictory, also,

01:18:36.420 --> 01:18:38.280
with the same
nature of how people

01:18:38.280 --> 01:18:41.520
might be able to
experience it, and to learn

01:18:41.520 --> 01:18:45.450
from a kind of a feedback
relationship between us.

01:18:45.450 --> 01:18:48.000
Or between how it's
used or misused.

01:18:48.000 --> 01:18:52.050
I thought maybe with
a [? meal, ?] sensing.

01:18:52.050 --> 01:18:54.000
And then it's much
more less responsible.

01:18:54.000 --> 01:18:55.950
Because when you
enter the space--

01:18:55.950 --> 01:18:57.870
and I'm always talking
about architecture--

01:18:57.870 --> 01:19:00.495
and they tell you all the things
that you can do and you cannot

01:19:00.495 --> 01:19:02.680
do, somehow you are enter
much more confident.

01:19:02.680 --> 01:19:04.680
Because you say, oh, they
told me I can do this,

01:19:04.680 --> 01:19:05.805
they told me I can do that.

01:19:05.805 --> 01:19:07.740
And then you move.

01:19:07.740 --> 01:19:10.410
The strategy I'm
always trying to avoid

01:19:10.410 --> 01:19:14.070
is like, don't tell nothing
to, the participant.

01:19:14.070 --> 01:19:17.370
When they enter there, they
are kind of, oh my god.

01:19:17.370 --> 01:19:18.630
What the hell is going on?

01:19:18.630 --> 01:19:20.880
You go [INAUDIBLE], you go down.

01:19:20.880 --> 01:19:23.820
Like somehow, the
learning process

01:19:23.820 --> 01:19:27.810
is much more your
responsibility toward the space

01:19:27.810 --> 01:19:30.210
or toward the planet,
[INAUDIBLE] that way.

01:19:30.210 --> 01:19:33.900
It's much more engaging,
and you work much better.

01:19:33.900 --> 01:19:34.920
It's much more attuned.

01:19:34.920 --> 01:19:36.945
It's much more sensible.

01:19:40.570 --> 01:19:42.760
So I had a question
about, the talk

01:19:42.760 --> 01:19:46.240
is called Beyond Materiality
and it's really fascinating.

01:19:46.240 --> 01:19:49.990
But so basically, so this the
space, which is the negative,

01:19:49.990 --> 01:19:53.450
then there's the solid object
which shapes the space.

01:19:53.450 --> 01:19:57.460
And if it's beyond
materiality, is it

01:19:57.460 --> 01:20:00.700
that materiality is [? wrap ?]
back around that the space is

01:20:00.700 --> 01:20:03.100
the primary investigation?

01:20:03.100 --> 01:20:05.830
And it seems like that's you're
really interested in the space.

01:20:05.830 --> 01:20:07.450
But like Nadia
was talking about,

01:20:07.450 --> 01:20:09.570
something has to shape
and define that space.

01:20:09.570 --> 01:20:12.040
And so, for example
the plastic sheets

01:20:12.040 --> 01:20:13.630
to find the different
layers, and how

01:20:13.630 --> 01:20:15.463
people moved with the
space close behind you

01:20:15.463 --> 01:20:18.190
was actually the mylar sheets
that would close behind you.

01:20:18.190 --> 01:20:21.340
So the materiality,
is it just whatever

01:20:21.340 --> 01:20:24.580
is needed to create
the environment?

01:20:24.580 --> 01:20:29.080
Or is it just that whatever
you can get your hands on,

01:20:29.080 --> 01:20:32.205
whatever is actually
does the job?

01:20:32.205 --> 01:20:33.580
Like I think there
was a question

01:20:33.580 --> 01:20:36.280
about what thickness
of thread you'd

01:20:36.280 --> 01:20:38.290
use for the spider web was.

01:20:38.290 --> 01:20:40.210
It just whatever was available?

01:20:40.210 --> 01:20:43.420
Or you said, OK, this is too
elastic, this is too stiff,

01:20:43.420 --> 01:20:44.780
so this one works.

01:20:44.780 --> 01:20:48.730
So it's just, there was no kind
of overt thought about, well,

01:20:48.730 --> 01:20:49.660
this is the material.

01:20:49.660 --> 01:20:52.540
Just that, well, this is what
works best to define the space

01:20:52.540 --> 01:20:56.545
and create the environment?

01:20:56.545 --> 01:20:57.045
Yes.

01:21:00.030 --> 01:21:00.910
No.

01:21:00.910 --> 01:21:05.700
About material, I'm here,
I enjoy, also, at MIT.

01:21:05.700 --> 01:21:09.540
I mean, we visit with
Leila, which was fantastic.

01:21:09.540 --> 01:21:12.480
Many experts on
different materials.

01:21:12.480 --> 01:21:16.500
For example, would be wonderful.

01:21:16.500 --> 01:21:21.370
But these nanographene tubes
are not still available to buy.

01:21:21.370 --> 01:21:23.180
Would love to have them.

01:21:23.180 --> 01:21:25.380
I'm trying to build, now,
a flying [? plastic. ?]

01:21:25.380 --> 01:21:28.050
There is this,
again this graphene.

01:21:28.050 --> 01:21:30.210
These sheets very, very light.

01:21:30.210 --> 01:21:32.617
If I will get these to
make a next solar balloon,

01:21:32.617 --> 01:21:34.200
it will be much more
smaller, and need

01:21:34.200 --> 01:21:38.200
less volume, less material,
but still is not available.

01:21:38.200 --> 01:21:40.590
This is a negotiation, always.

01:21:40.590 --> 01:21:42.540
I mean, the sheets
that I'm using,

01:21:42.540 --> 01:21:45.840
PVC, would be nice to
make them with ETFE

01:21:45.840 --> 01:21:50.220
which will last much longer.

01:21:50.220 --> 01:21:54.270
I don't know if I answered
exactly the question, but.

01:21:54.270 --> 01:21:56.520
Well, I guess it's just,
your main focus is the space.

01:21:56.520 --> 01:21:59.290
Whatever shapes the space
it's whatever it needs to be.

01:21:59.290 --> 01:21:59.790
Yes?

01:21:59.790 --> 01:22:00.150
Yes?

01:22:00.150 --> 01:22:00.650
Yeah.

01:22:00.650 --> 01:22:01.750
But let me put it again.

01:22:01.750 --> 01:22:04.230
For example, in
Museo Aero Solar,

01:22:04.230 --> 01:22:08.280
this kind of huge
museum, which fly

01:22:08.280 --> 01:22:11.070
made of reused plastic bags.

01:22:11.070 --> 01:22:13.530
Also the material over
there, you know, it's like,

01:22:13.530 --> 01:22:15.990
people bring it, or people
tape it at their home.

01:22:15.990 --> 01:22:17.430
And the space,
also, is something

01:22:17.430 --> 01:22:19.410
which is pretty
undefined, because it's

01:22:19.410 --> 01:22:21.840
due to collaboration
of the persons

01:22:21.840 --> 01:22:23.280
that the space keep growing.

01:22:23.280 --> 01:22:24.900
And the collection,
it keep growing.

01:22:24.900 --> 01:22:27.810
And somehow, let's say
put it in architecture,

01:22:27.810 --> 01:22:32.970
there's always this problem that
sometimes architecture build

01:22:32.970 --> 01:22:35.160
a museum that is too
big for the collection,

01:22:35.160 --> 01:22:36.780
or too small for
that collection.

01:22:36.780 --> 01:22:38.530
And you know, you don't
have enough space.

01:22:38.530 --> 01:22:41.680
Then you show these art and
you don't have to change.

01:22:41.680 --> 01:22:42.810
I mean, it's beautiful.

01:22:42.810 --> 01:22:46.270
This museum, somehow, always
show all the collection,

01:22:46.270 --> 01:22:47.832
which is plastic bags, somehow.

01:22:47.832 --> 01:22:50.040
And it keep growing, because
every time you add more,

01:22:50.040 --> 01:22:52.590
it kind of get bigger
and get bigger.

01:22:52.590 --> 01:22:54.480
And I think so.

01:22:54.480 --> 01:22:56.220
And that's also
like a space which

01:22:56.220 --> 01:22:58.860
just grow through the
participation of people.

01:22:58.860 --> 01:23:00.570
Or get small.

01:23:00.570 --> 01:23:02.460
Now, at the moment,
there are three museums.

01:23:02.460 --> 01:23:05.910
One in Helsinki, one in
Italy, and one in Germany.

01:23:05.910 --> 01:23:08.580
Sometimes what we do,
before there were many more,

01:23:08.580 --> 01:23:10.750
and now we glue them all
together and the space,

01:23:10.750 --> 01:23:11.640
it gets bigger.

01:23:11.640 --> 01:23:13.320
As bigger it gets,
the more lift is.

01:23:13.320 --> 01:23:15.820
There will be a moment maybe
we can carry a person up there,

01:23:15.820 --> 01:23:16.320
also.

01:23:16.320 --> 01:23:19.300
But it also change.

01:23:19.300 --> 01:23:23.760
And I think it is also
nice to, when you see it,

01:23:23.760 --> 01:23:25.440
you see every single piece.

01:23:25.440 --> 01:23:31.320
Somehow you get the idea of
how it's done, somehow, maybe.

01:23:31.320 --> 01:23:33.540
Of the materiality of it, no?

01:23:33.540 --> 01:23:35.910
Which is, you get really
personal, somehow.

01:23:35.910 --> 01:23:38.130
Over Santa Maria in
Colombia, Medellin.

01:23:38.130 --> 01:23:42.720
And every situation of
culture or scenario, it

01:23:42.720 --> 01:23:45.360
completely change, also,
how it's appropriated.

01:23:45.360 --> 01:23:46.950
Somehow Santa
Maria was in charge

01:23:46.950 --> 01:23:50.520
of many communities in
different favelas in Medellin.

01:23:50.520 --> 01:23:53.730
And he say, oh, you
know, we will ask

01:23:53.730 --> 01:23:57.180
and here worship with children.

01:23:57.180 --> 01:23:59.670
And then they were kind of
using to make drawings on top.

01:23:59.670 --> 01:24:01.590
And then everybody do something.

01:24:01.590 --> 01:24:04.530
Then when we were
Minneapolis and San Luis,

01:24:04.530 --> 01:24:05.913
because we wanted to let it fly.

01:24:05.913 --> 01:24:07.580
And then we start to
work with somebody,

01:24:07.580 --> 01:24:13.327
a retired person from NASA,
and then the FAA, Federal

01:24:13.327 --> 01:24:14.160
Aeronautic Aviation.

01:24:14.160 --> 01:24:16.620
To fly such a big
chunk of plastic,

01:24:16.620 --> 01:24:18.960
you have to have a
way to bring it down.

01:24:18.960 --> 01:24:23.580
This mean we build a sensor
to kind of [INAUDIBLE] it.

01:24:23.580 --> 01:24:25.020
Also space, you know.

01:24:25.020 --> 01:24:28.498
Because you have to fly but
not interfere with an airplane.

01:24:28.498 --> 01:24:29.790
That's another space, you know.

01:24:29.790 --> 01:24:30.840
The space you occupy.

01:24:38.810 --> 01:24:39.650
Yeah.

01:24:39.650 --> 01:24:41.240
I'd like to draw
attention to the fact

01:24:41.240 --> 01:24:43.640
that in almost every
picture we've seen,

01:24:43.640 --> 01:24:45.770
there's a human being.

01:24:45.770 --> 01:24:48.140
And it might be like
an obvious fact,

01:24:48.140 --> 01:24:52.730
but I think it's important to
stress it in the conversation.

01:24:52.730 --> 01:24:55.010
Because it addresses,
also, the thing

01:24:55.010 --> 01:24:57.350
of the audience,
the architecture,

01:24:57.350 --> 01:24:59.000
and the materiality.

01:24:59.000 --> 01:25:05.300
Because even in Venice with the
spiders, there's in the picture

01:25:05.300 --> 01:25:08.480
that we saw, there's this
guy standing next to it

01:25:08.480 --> 01:25:11.660
with little plastic
shoe protectors.

01:25:11.660 --> 01:25:15.950
And the shoe protector
is creating a distance

01:25:15.950 --> 01:25:20.630
from the floor, so that the
floor doesn't become a floor

01:25:20.630 --> 01:25:23.330
by getting some dirt on it.

01:25:23.330 --> 01:25:26.510
And so that we can still see the
white, which is the same white.

01:25:26.510 --> 01:25:28.700
So that the man who is
looking at the thing

01:25:28.700 --> 01:25:38.390
and is pondering this spider web
is like floating in that air.

01:25:38.390 --> 01:25:43.520
So I seem to identify this
importance of the human being

01:25:43.520 --> 01:25:47.750
that the balloon that is
floating with the sun, there's

01:25:47.750 --> 01:25:48.770
somebody hanging.

01:25:48.770 --> 01:25:51.590
There's always
somebody there being

01:25:51.590 --> 01:25:53.130
floated by the [INAUDIBLE].

01:25:53.130 --> 01:25:55.400
And so the question
of the materiality

01:25:55.400 --> 01:25:59.560
is also related to this
presence of the human being.

01:25:59.560 --> 01:26:02.550
Because the human being is
very real, very material,

01:26:02.550 --> 01:26:04.280
very there, very physical.

01:26:04.280 --> 01:26:08.900
And it is a whole
collection of devices

01:26:08.900 --> 01:26:16.100
to bring this physical person
into a new material atmosphere.

01:26:20.760 --> 01:26:22.500
Is that a conscious search?

01:26:22.500 --> 01:26:24.360
Or is it something
that I'm just seeing?

01:26:24.360 --> 01:26:27.390
No, no, I love people.

01:26:27.390 --> 01:26:28.232
Still.

01:26:28.232 --> 01:26:34.080
[CHUCKLES] No, no, no.

01:26:34.080 --> 01:26:36.210
But I'm disappointed, you know.

01:26:36.210 --> 01:26:40.710
I think tomorrow is, no, when
is the homage to Enrico Fermi?

01:26:40.710 --> 01:26:42.510
That Friedman had--

01:26:42.510 --> 01:26:44.090
Monday.

01:26:44.090 --> 01:26:46.650
Monday, 3:30.

01:26:46.650 --> 01:26:49.110
Somebody have wrote a book
based on one of the sentence

01:26:49.110 --> 01:26:50.430
that he always said.

01:26:50.430 --> 01:26:52.080
Where is everybody?

01:26:52.080 --> 01:26:53.490
Right?

01:26:53.490 --> 01:26:57.120
Where is everybody?

01:26:57.120 --> 01:26:59.310
And then I will tell
you, now, a story

01:26:59.310 --> 01:27:02.080
which kind of rephrased,
maybe the same, which I think

01:27:02.080 --> 01:27:04.050
is a story by
[? Stanis ?] [INAUDIBLE]

01:27:04.050 --> 01:27:06.210
written in Carl Sagan's book.

01:27:06.210 --> 01:27:08.970
I don't know if it's Voyager
or some of the other one.

01:27:08.970 --> 01:27:10.860
And then there are people on--

01:27:10.860 --> 01:27:12.900
always about people,
I'm telling you-- there

01:27:12.900 --> 01:27:16.170
are people on the planet Earth
trying to see if there is life

01:27:16.170 --> 01:27:18.600
or there are other
people in another planet.

01:27:18.600 --> 01:27:20.940
And then the first,
what they do,

01:27:20.940 --> 01:27:23.400
there are some astronomers
[INAUDIBLE],, they

01:27:23.400 --> 01:27:25.560
build the telescope
of that science, no?

01:27:25.560 --> 01:27:28.080
And then they look and say,
oh, there is nobody up there.

01:27:28.080 --> 01:27:31.390
Then next year, they build a
telescope a little bit bigger.

01:27:31.390 --> 01:27:33.465
They look again, oh,
there is nobody up there.

01:27:33.465 --> 01:27:36.090
Then the next year, they make it
a little bit bigger like this.

01:27:36.090 --> 01:27:37.440
They look, and nothing.

01:27:37.440 --> 01:27:39.730
And then the telescope keep
growing, growing, growing.

01:27:39.730 --> 01:27:42.540
Now, there is a moment that
the telescope is so big

01:27:42.540 --> 01:27:44.758
that the other ones see
first the telescope,

01:27:44.758 --> 01:27:46.800
and we, through the
telescope, see the other one.

01:27:46.800 --> 01:27:49.020
You know what I mean?

01:27:49.020 --> 01:27:51.510
You know, and I
love this idea of,

01:27:51.510 --> 01:27:53.460
it's again, where is everybody?

01:27:53.460 --> 01:27:59.200
You know, I wish we might be
able to take into account more.

01:27:59.200 --> 01:28:02.830
Even beyond this planet.

01:28:02.830 --> 01:28:05.328
And maybe, I don't know.

01:28:09.190 --> 01:28:10.660
Thank you.

01:28:10.660 --> 01:28:15.610
I have a two-part question
about the spider web in Venice.

01:28:15.610 --> 01:28:18.100
The first is a very MIT
kind of tech question,

01:28:18.100 --> 01:28:21.010
and I'll ask the
other one later.

01:28:21.010 --> 01:28:24.700
The structure, once
you have the map

01:28:24.700 --> 01:28:27.280
of what the spider
did, and you have

01:28:27.280 --> 01:28:29.710
your thing you
wish to create, you

01:28:29.710 --> 01:28:31.840
have so many degrees of freedom.

01:28:31.840 --> 01:28:34.240
It's very, very
difficult to make it.

01:28:34.240 --> 01:28:36.730
So as you were
putting it together,

01:28:36.730 --> 01:28:38.320
what was more important to you?

01:28:38.320 --> 01:28:39.790
What was your priority?

01:28:39.790 --> 01:28:42.250
The topology, in
other words, this node

01:28:42.250 --> 01:28:45.190
is connected to this node
is connected to that node,

01:28:45.190 --> 01:28:49.100
or where the node
ended up in space?

01:28:49.100 --> 01:28:50.980
Well, that's another question.

01:28:54.620 --> 01:28:56.960
No, I was pretty much
obsessed with trying to build

01:28:56.960 --> 01:28:58.550
as perfect as possible.

01:28:58.550 --> 01:29:03.060
You know, I don't know if I
answered the first question.

01:29:03.060 --> 01:29:06.710
And then the other, I think so.

01:29:06.710 --> 01:29:09.230
I always think it's
like, it was an exercise.

01:29:09.230 --> 01:29:12.230
Just, I wanted this node
[INAUDIBLE] happy go here.

01:29:12.230 --> 01:29:14.090
And from here, it go here.

01:29:14.090 --> 01:29:16.790
So you kept going until
everything was right?

01:29:16.790 --> 01:29:19.290
Yeah, I want to become
a kind of Spider Man.

01:29:19.290 --> 01:29:20.060
OK.

01:29:20.060 --> 01:29:24.020
So the second part of the
question is more philosophical.

01:29:24.020 --> 01:29:29.930
For you, what was the most
important part of the art?

01:29:29.930 --> 01:29:34.560
Developing the method to make
the spider web, or the finished

01:29:34.560 --> 01:29:35.060
web?

01:29:39.320 --> 01:29:41.420
The art, for me, was
really like to build

01:29:41.420 --> 01:29:43.370
this web between
relationship with people.

01:29:43.370 --> 01:29:45.830
Again, maybe the people
were not so visible.

01:29:45.830 --> 01:29:48.320
But they can have a beautiful
spirit of collaboration

01:29:48.320 --> 01:29:50.250
that we weave between us.

01:29:50.250 --> 01:29:52.070
That was really the work.

01:29:52.070 --> 01:29:53.780
Which is invisible, again.

01:29:53.780 --> 01:29:57.530
But I think so that really
drives me completely.

01:29:57.530 --> 01:29:59.330
I mean, was really beautiful.

01:29:59.330 --> 01:30:00.770
Which is not even in the world.

01:30:00.770 --> 01:30:03.920
But somehow show that this was
possible, thanks to something

01:30:03.920 --> 01:30:05.810
which is unaccountable.

01:30:05.810 --> 01:30:07.890
New friendship or
whatever it is.

01:30:07.890 --> 01:30:10.670
But I think so that's really,
that's really the work.

01:30:10.670 --> 01:30:13.100
I think so.

01:30:13.100 --> 01:30:13.600
Thank you.

01:30:17.070 --> 01:30:22.620
I have a question,
which is, you've

01:30:22.620 --> 01:30:25.620
talked a lot about of the
importance of the sort

01:30:25.620 --> 01:30:28.290
of social interaction,
and the sort

01:30:28.290 --> 01:30:30.060
of people occupying the space.

01:30:30.060 --> 01:30:33.750
But you've also
mentioned briefly

01:30:33.750 --> 01:30:36.720
how the institutions where
you're placing these works

01:30:36.720 --> 01:30:39.360
have asked you to
make compromises

01:30:39.360 --> 01:30:41.370
in how people are using them.

01:30:41.370 --> 01:30:45.540
When and how do you
decide what compromises

01:30:45.540 --> 01:30:47.130
you're willing to make?

01:30:50.870 --> 01:31:01.780
Is always a very, like
you sense it yourself,

01:31:01.780 --> 01:31:04.230
until something they ask
you, no, no, no, no, then

01:31:04.230 --> 01:31:05.940
one moment you explode
and say OK, basta,

01:31:05.940 --> 01:31:08.350
I don't want to do it, you know.

01:31:08.350 --> 01:31:12.540
But otherwise, you always
accept something, some note.

01:31:12.540 --> 01:31:15.030
But mostly, the things
like what I try to do

01:31:15.030 --> 01:31:19.900
is never being myself, and
then there is the institution.

01:31:19.900 --> 01:31:22.658
And so we work together
right from the beginning.

01:31:22.658 --> 01:31:24.450
Because mostly our
project, which are like,

01:31:24.450 --> 01:31:29.210
it costs 30 times more
budget that they offer me.

01:31:29.210 --> 01:31:32.350
I mean, if they don't want
to do it from their side,

01:31:32.350 --> 01:31:35.340
it's almost impossible, right
from the beginning, to do it.

01:31:35.340 --> 01:31:37.080
because it's really
like a, I mean,

01:31:37.080 --> 01:31:39.077
they're very, very engaged
from the beginning.

01:31:39.077 --> 01:31:41.160
Because usually, it's a
little bit more ambitious,

01:31:41.160 --> 01:31:44.220
and there are these kind of
architecture scale in some

01:31:44.220 --> 01:31:48.687
of them, which kind of also.

01:31:48.687 --> 01:31:50.520
And sometimes the
institution, also, they're

01:31:50.520 --> 01:31:54.150
not so used to work at that
scale, which also kind of get

01:31:54.150 --> 01:31:58.180
a lot of trouble, also,
for curators and museum

01:31:58.180 --> 01:32:00.780
and institutions, because
it's like, oh, my god.

01:32:00.780 --> 01:32:03.510
And me also, somehow, because
I had never really practiced

01:32:03.510 --> 01:32:06.870
architecture so straightforward.

01:32:06.870 --> 01:32:11.130
It come together,
somehow, always.

01:32:11.130 --> 01:32:15.311
So are you seeing your art is in
upping the budget thirty-fold?

01:32:15.311 --> 01:32:16.290
[LAUGHTER]

01:32:16.290 --> 01:32:17.040
Don't say this.

01:32:17.040 --> 01:32:19.015
You said [INAUDIBLE] commission.

01:32:24.240 --> 01:32:26.670
Well, we have time
for one more question,

01:32:26.670 --> 01:32:28.140
and then we need to wrap it up.

01:32:34.300 --> 01:32:35.050
Hi, hello.

01:32:35.050 --> 01:32:35.770
OK.

01:32:35.770 --> 01:32:38.020
I discovered you on
Sunday, and I'm not

01:32:38.020 --> 01:32:41.560
affiliated with the school
or architecture at all.

01:32:41.560 --> 01:32:45.790
So I see that your work
exists in outside space

01:32:45.790 --> 01:32:47.530
and inside space.

01:32:47.530 --> 01:32:52.510
Do you enjoy, or are
you more inspired by,

01:32:52.510 --> 01:32:55.480
a space that's really vast
with so much potential?

01:32:55.480 --> 01:32:58.180
Or do you enjoy the
limiting spaces that

01:32:58.180 --> 01:33:00.880
are smaller or in interior?

01:33:00.880 --> 01:33:01.570
Yeah.

01:33:01.570 --> 01:33:03.070
No, both, I think so.

01:33:03.070 --> 01:33:05.170
And always, you know, the
space inside your brain

01:33:05.170 --> 01:33:06.970
is like a [INAUDIBLE].

01:33:06.970 --> 01:33:12.370
There is always this analogy
also, the spider web,

01:33:12.370 --> 01:33:14.920
I think so, we are visiting
somebody who is specializing

01:33:14.920 --> 01:33:17.440
in [? connections ?] on Monday.

01:33:17.440 --> 01:33:23.730
And then is kind of the
inner connectivity of cells.

01:33:23.730 --> 01:33:31.090
And this means, I think so, it's
kind of this simultaneously,

01:33:31.090 --> 01:33:34.900
you know, simultaneity
between the appreciation

01:33:34.900 --> 01:33:42.290
of these multiple scales which
kind of make me enthusiastic.

01:33:42.290 --> 01:33:44.950
And then when it doesn't
work on one scale,

01:33:44.950 --> 01:33:47.660
I say, well, it might
work in some other scale

01:33:47.660 --> 01:33:51.360
inside my brain, or inside
the brain of somebody else.

01:33:51.360 --> 01:33:53.800
Or something like this.

01:33:53.800 --> 01:33:56.680
Tomas, you've been very
generous with your time

01:33:56.680 --> 01:33:57.490
and your thinking.

01:33:57.490 --> 01:34:00.140
You're still here, so people
have more access to them.

01:34:00.140 --> 01:34:02.770
I just want to thank you again
for this wonderful event.

01:34:02.770 --> 01:34:03.770
Thanks to all of you.

01:34:03.770 --> 01:34:07.420
[APPLAUSE]

